## Key Design Decisions

### 1. Embedded Broker (vs. External)
**Decision**: Run Moquette inside Android app

**Why?**
- **Simplicity**: No separate broker to install/maintain
- **Offline**: Works without internet
- **Cost**: No broker hosting fees
- **Latency**: <10ms (localhost) vs. 200-500ms (cloud)

**Trade-off**:
- Tablet becomes single point of failure
- Limited to ~50 concurrent scanners
- Requires tablet to stay powered

**Mitigation**:
- Watchdog for auto-restart
- Battery optimization recommendations
- Support multiple tablets per site (future)

### 2. Zone-Based (vs. GPS/Trilateration)
**Decision**: Strongest RSSI determines zone

**Why?**
- **Indoor-Friendly**: GPS doesn't work indoors
- **Simple**: No complex math (trilateration unreliable with RSSI)
- **Privacy**: Room-level accuracy (not XY coordinates)
- **Scalable**: Easy to add zones

**Trade-off**:
- Room-level accuracy only (not sub-meter)
- Requires 1 scanner per zone
- RSSI can fluctuate

**Acceptable Because**:
- "In Room A" is sufficient for 90% of use cases
- Can add more scanners for finer granularity
- Dwell time filter reduces false transitions

### 3. Foreground Service (vs. Background)
**Decision**: Run as foreground service with persistent notification

**Why?**
- **Reliability**: Android kills background services
- **User Visibility**: Notification shows system is active
- **Auto-Restart**: System restarts on crash (START_STICKY)

**Trade-off**:
- Persistent notification (can't hide)
- Higher battery usage
- Notification occupies status bar

**Acceptable Because**:
- 24/7 operation requires foreground priority
- Notification builds trust (users know it's working)
- Battery life still 24-48 hours

### 4. Auto-Provisioning (vs. Manual)
**Decision**: UDP discovery + HTTP config push

**Why?**
- **User-Friendly**: No SSH, no terminal
- **Fast**: 2 minutes vs. 10 minutes per scanner
- **Scalable**: Bulk provision 50 scanners
- **Zero-Config**: Scanners auto-discover tablet

**Trade-off**:
- HTTP (not HTTPS) on setup network
- Requires temporary "setup" WiFi
- Scanner must be in range during provisioning

**Acceptable Because**:
- Setup network is isolated (no internet)
- Short exposure window (5 minutes)
- Manual approval required (not fully automatic)

### 5. MQTT QoS 1 (vs. QoS 0 or 2)
**Decision**: At-least-once delivery

**Why?**
- **Reliable**: Acknowledged by broker (no silent loss)
- **Performant**: Less overhead than QoS 2
- **Acceptable Duplicates**: <0.5%, filtered at app layer

**Trade-off**:
- Possible duplicates (rare)
- Slightly higher bandwidth than QoS 0

**Acceptable Because**:
- Asset tracking needs reliability (QoS 0 too risky)
- QoS 2 overkill for beacon data
- Duplicates handled by timestamp + MAC cache

